{
  "slug": "find-k-closest-elements",
  "title": "K Closest Values to x",
  "topic": "binary-search",
  "difficulty": "Medium",
  "pattern": "binary search on the starting index of a fixed-length window",
  "statement": "`nums` is an array of integers, handed over in any order. Choose `k` of its values that are closest to `x`, where the distance between a value `v` and `x` is `|v - x|`. When two values are equally distant from `x` the smaller value is preferred, which fixes exactly which values are chosen; a value that appears several times in `nums` can be chosen several times. Return the chosen values in ascending order.\n\nFor example `nums = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]`, `k = 4` and `x = 3` gives `[1, 2, 3, 4]`: the distances are `2, 1, 0, 1, 2`, and once `2`, `3` and `4` are kept the last place goes to `1` rather than `5` because the two are equally distant and `1` is smaller.\n\nConstraints: `1 <= nums.length <= 200`, `1 <= k <= nums.length`, `-10000 <= nums[i] <= 10000`, `-10000 <= x <= 10000`.\n",
  "examples": [
    {
      "input": "nums = [-10000], k = 1, x = -10000",
      "output": "[-10000]",
      "explanation": "Produced by the reference solution, verified against all four implementations."
    },
    {
      "input": "nums = [-8357, 9142], k = 2, x = 6477",
      "output": "[-8357, 9142]",
      "explanation": "Produced by the reference solution, verified against all four implementations."
    }
  ],
  "constraints": [
    "Function signature: `function solve(nums: number[], k: number, x: number)`.",
    "Inputs are JSON-serializable values satisfying the bounds stated above."
  ],
  "hints": [
    "Sorting every value by its distance to x and taking the first k is the direct answer; the only subtlety is which value wins when two distances are equal.",
    "Once the values are in ascending order, the chosen ones are always one block of length k, so the whole answer is decided by where that block starts.",
    "Judge a candidate block by the two values just outside it: the farther one is the value the block should drop, and the comparison is monotone, so bisect over the possible starts."
  ],
  "editorial": {
    "approach": "Rank the values by the pair (distance to x, value): the smaller value wins a tie, so this is a total order and the answers are exactly the k smallest values in it. The brute force computes every value's rank by comparing it with all the others, which is O(n^2), and keeps the values whose rank is below k before sorting the survivors ascending. The optimal solution starts from the fact that, in ascending order, the chosen values are always one unbroken block of length k, so the only open question is where that block begins. Comparing the value just left of a candidate block with the value just right of it settles the matter: the block slides right only when the left value is strictly farther from x, so an equal distance keeps the smaller value exactly as the tie-break requires. That decision is monotone in the block's start, so a binary search over the n - k + 1 possible starts locates it in O(log n) probes.",
    "why_optimal": "The array arrives unordered and the chosen values are a contiguous block only after it is ordered, so the optimal solution pays one O(n log n) sort and then bisects, which is what lets it ignore the brute force's pairwise counting. The decision at one start is monotone because once the left value stops being farther away no later start can win, which is the property that makes halving valid. Copying out the k answers is unavoidable, so O(n log n + k) is the tight bound for this route, and the extra space is the ordered copy plus the answer.",
    "pitfalls": "Reading the array as already ordered: it is not, and the window bisection is only meaningful on an ordered copy. Ranking by distance alone and leaving the tie between two equally distant values to chance instead of preferring the smaller value. Getting the tie direction backwards and sliding right when the distances are equal, which silently picks the larger value. Setting the high end to the array's length and reading one element past the end of the candidate block, when the last valid start is n - k. Sorting before the final copy when the ordered copy already hands the k chosen values back in ascending order."
  },
